      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[0,4].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [0,4]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*",
      "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,4],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(0)=0$ and $f(4)=14$, we must have \n$k\\in [0,14].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$0$ and $14$\".",
